{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good-cause requirement","requirement":"A landlord may recover possession only by tenant consent or through the legal eviction process, and may not evict or fail to renew a lease without good cause. A Notice to Quit is required for every good-cause eviction except nonpayment of rent.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential landlords and tenants; applies to dwelling units used as a residence, under written or oral leases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential rental dwelling units"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.85,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No statewide rent control; municipal rent leveling permitted","requirement":"New Jersey has no state law establishing, governing or controlling rents; municipalities may adopt rent control or rent leveling ordinances, enforced by locally created boards.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental housing in New Jersey; whether a unit is covered depends on whether its municipality has adopted a rent control or rent leveling ordinance.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Coverage set by municipal ordinance"},"exemptions":"Housing owned or subsidized by HUD, HUD-insured developments HUD finds to have economic problems, and NJHMFA-financed projects are not subject to municipal rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J. Dep't of Community Affairs, Landlord-Tenant Publication, 'Rent Control/Rent Increases'","quoted_span":"The State of New Jersey has no laws that establish, govern or control rents. Municipalities may pass an ordinance establishing rent control or rent leveling.","interaction":"Municipal rent control and rent leveling ordinances operate where the State has no rent control law.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law 30-year rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from any local rent control ordinance for 30 years after construction of the building is completed.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings; the exemption runs for 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Exemption runs 30 years from completion of construction"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rent increase must not be unconscionable; notice before eviction for increased rent","requirement":"Before evicting for nonpayment of an increased rent, the landlord must serve a valid notice to quit and notice of rent increase; the increase must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal rent ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies; the increase must comply with any municipal ordinance governing rent increases.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"If the increase is unconscionable or proper notice was not given, the tenant may file a complaint with a municipal rent control board.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"The increase must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.85,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"No rent increase for conversion costs","requirement":"Where a building is converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in a building converted to condominium or cooperative ownership or fee simple ownership of units, whether or not eligible for protected tenancy.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Rents may still be increased to cover the cost of new services or amenities.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.85,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New owner responsible for transferred security deposit and interest","requirement":"When a rental property is sold or transferred, the new owner must obtain the tenant's security deposit and accrued interest from the former owner and is responsible for investing the deposit, giving notices, paying interest and returning the deposit with earnings.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential tenancies where the rental property is sold or transferred.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"If the property is sold or transferred it is the duty of the new owner to obtain the security deposit, plus accrued interest on the tenant’s deposit, that was collected by the former owner.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Civil penalty for willfully withholding deposit of assisted tenant","requirement":"A landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit of a tenant receiving state or federal financial assistance, including welfare or rental assistance, may be liable for a civil penalty per offense.","key_value":"$500 to $2,000 per offense","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who received financial assistance through any state or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance, whose deposits were willfully and intentionally withheld.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense, enforced by summary proceedings; amounts recovered go to the state entity that made the deposits.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21.1","quoted_span":"The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Criminal penalty for unlawfully using security deposit monies","requirement":"A person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be charged as a disorderly person and fined or imprisoned.","key_value":"$200 fine or 30 days","coverage_conditions":"Any person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-25","quoted_span":"Any person who unlawfully uses security deposit monies may be c riminally charged as a disorderly person and may be subject to a fine of not less than $200 or imprisonment for not more than 30 days, or both (N.J.S.A. 46:8-25).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Landlord may charge for credit report and reasonable application fees","requirement":"A landlord may check an applicant's credit and background and may charge the applicant the cost of the credit report and request reasonable rental application fees; if the application is denied based on the credit report, the landlord must give the agency's name, address and telephone number.","key_value":"cost of credit report; reasonable application fees","coverage_conditions":"Rental applicants screened by landlords through credit bureaus, tenant screening agencies or public records.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"15 U.S.C.A. § 1681m","quoted_span":"The landlord is allowed to charge the tenant for the cost of the report. The landlord may also request reasonable rental application fees or employment verification.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Law Against Discrimination source-of-lawful-income protection","requirement":"A landlord or rental agency may not refuse to rent or discriminate in renting housing because of a person's source of lawful income, among the other characteristics listed in the Law Against Discrimination.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships in New Jersey, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home, and single-sex or age-restricted housing as to familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Two-family owner-occupied and owner-occupied room rentals exempt"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in an owner- or resident-occupied single home; residences planned for and occupied by one sex (e.g. YMCA) and age-restricted housing as to familial status.","penalty":"Under the LAD violators are subject to fines up to $10,000 for a first offense, and may owe monetary damages and be required to rent the unit to the complainant.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
